WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.650
[TRADITIONAL MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.610
STUDENT: First I should add water.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:10.330
Measure [INAUDIBLE] cup.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.900
[SPEAKING IN TURKISH]

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:23.760
[MUSIC]

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.830
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: I was
born in Baku, Azerbaijan.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.030
My parents are from a small tribe
in Azerbaijan called Juhuri,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.090
and they immigrated when I was
four from Azerbaijan to Turkey.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.660
So for the biggest chunk of my
life I grew up in Istanbul, Turkey.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.860
And had a great time, learned
a lot about Turkish culture.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.820
I wanted to work over summer.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.230
And one of the best work
opportunities for people my age

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.350
who speak some kind
of English was to work

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.985
as a fortune teller for the tourists.

00:00:50.985 --> 00:00:51.960
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Oh, can we have some coffee?

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:52.240
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Yes.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:52.720
Let's start.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:52.990
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Yeah?

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:53.230
OK.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.605
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: So let's, yeah.

00:00:57.444 --> 00:00:58.860
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Oh, it's delicious, yeah.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.020
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: It's great.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:00.590
ALYSSA GOODMAN: How do we do this?

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:01.465
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: OK.

00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:03.340
So first we're going
to take the coffee cup.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.490
And then we're going to
cover it with the saucer.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.050
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK, got it.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.390
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And then
with your thumb at the bottom

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.792
and then your index finger
middle finger on the top--

00:01:13.792 --> 00:01:14.750
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK, like this?

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:15.750
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: --yes.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.950
And now you're going to make a wish
that you're not going to tell us.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.170
And keep about that
wish and how much you

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.590
want this wish to happen in real life.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.140
And while doing it, turn
three times clockwise.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:26.640
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.066
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: OK?

00:01:32.066 --> 00:01:33.940
And then now we're going
to very quickly turn

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.540
it outwards and put its
saucer on the table.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.250
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.780
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And
while we're doing this,

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.870
I'm going to say a Turkish
phrase that basically

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.840
says whatever is my situation,
let that appear in the cup.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.300
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.000
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: [SPEAKING TURKISH] OK.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:51.580
Great.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.413
Now we're going to let
it hold a little bit.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:57.286
So before we do that, would you
mind putting your ring or a coin.

00:01:57.286 --> 00:01:59.160
The ring indicates that
you want to hear more

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.180
about personal relationships,
romantic relationships, friends, love,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.145
sort of things.

00:02:05.145 --> 00:02:10.419
A coin indicates you want to know more
about your professional goals, money,

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:11.125
bank accounts.

00:02:13.686 --> 00:02:14.810
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Let's go personal.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:15.240
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: OK.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:15.930
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK, Put the coin--

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.750
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Just put it on the--

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:17.290
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Put it on there?

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:17.670
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Yeah.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.170
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.950
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And we believe
that the energy of the ring

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.490
and what the ring stands for
is going to actually influence

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.379
what is going to be seen in the cup.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.670
It's not only about the future.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:31.799
It's also about what's present and the
past, because it nurtures our knowledge

00:02:31.799 --> 00:02:33.340
of what is future going to be, right?

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.086
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Right.

00:02:34.086 --> 00:02:37.250
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: We'll wait
a little bit until it dries.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:41.480
The fortune teller obviously, like
all the types of other fortunetelling,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.300
a lot of them, you try to
read your customer in a way.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.310
So at first what happens-- what were
my instructions in the cafe-- was

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.260
the tourists would come,
I would ask tourists

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.676
a bunch of introductory questions.

00:02:52.676 --> 00:02:57.880
Try to understand whether this person
is rich or poor or married or single.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.600
Where is this person from?

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.630
And kind of understand what are
the worries in this person's mind?

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:06.320
And after you drink the coffee and
try to start interpreting the coffee,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.560
you kind of project the things
you've learned about this person into

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.020
and the coffee cup and
pretend that it's the coffee

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.150
cup that's telling you all those
things that you have learned before.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.230
So you really use your
interpretation skills in ways.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.270
It's almost like a clinical
psychology practice,

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.970
where you are trying to bring--

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.490
without giving directed
advice to individuals,

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:29.037
you're trying to lead them to come to
advice they want to hear by themselves,

00:03:29.037 --> 00:03:29.870
and they want to do.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.994
So I think some of the
fortune tellers would really

00:03:31.994 --> 00:03:33.650
approach their work from that lens.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.210
But we also used to have a booklet.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.090
And the booklet basically was--

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.332
this was a short, maybe
10 to 15 pages, booklet.

00:03:39.332 --> 00:03:40.790
But it would say rabbit means this.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:42.000
Star means that.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.810
A skyscraper means this, et cetera.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:49.340
So this way when we interpret the
shapes, we have kind of a basis.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.572
A lot of people have different
ways of looking at the cup inside.

00:03:52.572 --> 00:03:54.280
Because now you're
going to see I'm going

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.040
to divide the cup in different parts.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.740
And different parts for me is
going to mean different things.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:04.440
So I'll put my cup away, and I
will return your ring to you.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.610
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Thank you.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:06.290
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: The energy is there.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:07.248
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Oh, I feel it.

00:04:07.248 --> 00:04:12.360
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And I
will flip the cup over, ooh.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:17.850
The way I look at the cup is always that
basically the handle parts, this part,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.070
is about love.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.470
This part is about wealth.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.400
When I cut from the middle, the
left part is going to be present

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.150
and the past, and then the right
part is going to be about the future.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.890
And the very dark bottom is
going to be about family.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.620
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Some other
people at Radcliffe here

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.620
also had this discussion about
whether people really, really want

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.732
to know the future or if they just
kind of want to chat about the future.

00:04:43.732 --> 00:04:45.690
And you know maybe what
you're saying is great,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.910
or maybe it's just a nice
opportunity to have a chat.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:51.230
So what was the balance of
that kind of goal with people?

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.730
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: I think for
our culture, most of the time,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.660
it's a social tool to get to know
other people and catch up with friends.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.290
So when you say, let's have
a cup of Turkish coffee,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.310
this means that you're committing for a
chunk of an hour and a half, two hours

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.232
to actually sit down.

00:05:06.232 --> 00:05:07.440
It's not something on the go.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.080
And you sit down and really
discuss life matters, right?

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.310
Let's say you are divorcing from someone
or you are changing your career path,

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.620
you're relocating from
one city to another,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.780
or your mother is going
through an illness, kind

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.820
of important events in your life.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.750
And you want those events to be
discussed and mentioned and shared

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:28.530
with someone.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.113
But you also want to know whether
those events are potentially

00:05:31.113 --> 00:05:33.220
going to come to a positive
or a negative outcome.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:37.380
So I think coffee drinking culture
and the coffee cup fortunetelling

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.882
really enables that type of a venue for
people to alleviate their own worries.

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:42.340
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Do you know how old it is?

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.340
When it started?

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.131
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Yes,
so we know according

00:05:45.131 --> 00:05:48.770
to historical records of the Ottoman
Empire that it's around 16th century

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.890
when the Ottoman Sultan for the
first time started having this.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.460
What we call today the Turkish
coffee actually comes from Yemen.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.370
And it was imported
into Ottoman times when

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.762
it basically was a
part of Ottoman Empire,

00:06:00.762 --> 00:06:02.220
and came all the way to the Sultan.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.210
And Sultan loved the taste of it.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:07.710
And we know that some of the
people were brought from the city,

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:10.380
from Istanbul at the time,
to come to the Sultan

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.230
and give his fortune
from looking at the cup.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.860
And that's how it spread to the
local elite of the Ottoman Empire

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:17.740
at the time.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.820
And from there spread
outwards to all the region.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.110
I see a volcano.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.640
ALYSSA GOODMAN: A volcano?

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:24.896
Oh, look at that!

00:06:24.896 --> 00:06:28.100
That really looks almost
like a pyramid volcano.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.637
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: See
there's some lava coming.

00:06:30.637 --> 00:06:33.720
Actually a volcano is a very common
feature in coffee cup fortune telling.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.450
It is a mountain and a volcano.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.375
A volcano happens when
the top is not sharp.

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:39.000
ALYSSA GOODMAN: I see.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.810
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And a mountain
happens when the top is sharp.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:41.770
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:43.728
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Mountain
means actually there

00:06:43.728 --> 00:06:46.590
is going to be a challenging
path, and at the end,

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.680
there is going to be a lot of
relief and a lot of happiness.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.620
Volcano means there's a lot of something
coming, it's like a volcano eruption.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.380
That might mean good
things or bad things.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:06:59.220
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Great.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:02.850
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: But
mostly volcanoes indicate

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:06.780
that there's a lot of energy of
some sort that's coming your way.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.372
And this volcano,
gladly, has an opening.

00:07:10.372 --> 00:07:12.330
Do you see that at the
end there is an opening?

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:13.170
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Definitely.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.810
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: So this opening
would indicate, once again,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.325
that whether this volcano brings
you bad news or good news,

00:07:18.325 --> 00:07:20.200
whether there's a lot
of bad things happening

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:24.370
or a lot of good things happening in one
moment, it is going to result in good.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:24.930
ALYSSA GOODMAN: OK.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.660
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: If this was completely
closed which could have happened,

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.719
if that was completely
closed that would mean

00:07:30.719 --> 00:07:32.760
that it's just going to
be negative results, even

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.710
if it's a positive volcano.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:37.329
So this would mean nothing to worry.

00:07:37.329 --> 00:07:39.870
There's going to be a time that's
coming in the near future--

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.997
and we also know that coffee cups
cannot tell you more than 40 days.

00:07:42.997 --> 00:07:43.830
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Oh, really?

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.871
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: So this
is in the next 40 days.

00:07:45.871 --> 00:07:48.450
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Is that to ensure
the return of the patron

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.150
to the coffee fortune teller?

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.920
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: It means that
I want to see my friend again.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.070
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Got it, OK.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:56.145
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: In
no more than 40 days.

00:07:56.145 --> 00:07:58.020
When I was growing up
and my mom was working,

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.070
and I was around nine
years old, 10 years old,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.640
and didn't want to stay alone, I
would go with my mom to the work

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.556
and see all these women doing these
different types of fortune telling

00:08:05.556 --> 00:08:09.590
and prediction systems, echoing with
the cycles, lunar cycles or astrology,

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:13.390
about what's going to happen in
certain Turkish people's lives.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.660
ALYSSA GOODMAN: And that was a big
part of the local culture?

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.280
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Yeah, it was
a big part of the local culture

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.070
and I think there's definitely
certain individuals who

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:21.691
choose to not engage in it.

00:08:21.691 --> 00:08:24.440
And there's certain individuals
who choose to believe in it a lot.

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:27.930
In Azeri, we have a phrase
that says, don't ever

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:31.340
believe in fortune telling fully, but
never stay without fortune telling.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.132
So you need to do it a
little bit, but not too much.

00:08:34.132 --> 00:08:36.090
If you see there's a
bunch of towers, actually.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.340
This could be an Eiffel Tower.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.186
ALYSSA GOODMAN: I am going on the Eiffel Tower.

00:08:39.186 --> 00:08:41.394
I've never-- been to France
many times and I've never

00:08:41.394 --> 00:08:42.999
gone up in the Eiffel Tower.

00:08:42.999 --> 00:08:44.790
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And
the Eiffel Tower is--

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:45.980
oh, my god, the Eiffel Tower--

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.370
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Looks exactly
like the Eiffel Tower.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:48.780
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: It does exactly
look like the Eiffel Tower.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.738
Can you turn that so that
we can see a picture?

00:08:50.738 --> 00:08:54.540
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: And the Eiffel
Tower is in the romantic side.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:55.130
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Oh.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:55.963
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:08:55.963 --> 00:08:57.960
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Oh, come on, it's Paris.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.480
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: It
is a city of romance.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.600
So the main situation of
where I see coffee being drank

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.780
is the middle age population, because
they have the biggest worries,

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:09.870
I think, in their lives about
money, about love, about marriage,

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.080
about different things.

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.370
So they usually see this as a
tool that they can believe in.

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:16.401
They still consume Turkish coffee a lot.

00:09:16.401 --> 00:09:18.400
A lot of times the people
who are going to do it

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.566
are the people who have the time to be
able to commute two hours to a friend

00:09:21.566 --> 00:09:22.554
and sit down.

00:09:22.554 --> 00:09:24.970
ALYSSA GOODMAN: You were also saying
something about people don't

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:26.440
drink the coffee for the caffeine?

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:27.070
Is that what you said before?

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.450
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: Yeah, totally.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.260
I think--

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:29.820
ALYSSA GOODMAN: So it's a purely social event.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:30.430
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: 100%.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.260
No one would drink Turkish
coffee because they

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:35.135
want to stay awake that night, which
I feel like people in the States

00:09:35.135 --> 00:09:38.270
and at Harvard, specifically, we do
it like, oh, I need to wake up today.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:39.640
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Does it have a lot of caffeine?

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:40.600
Or I'm going to find out later.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:41.690
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: It
has a lot of caffeine.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.898
And I think maybe some people
would find it offensive

00:09:43.898 --> 00:09:47.942
if you drink this coffee in a rush
to just keep yourself awakened.

00:09:47.942 --> 00:09:50.650
Because it is an offense to the
tradition of drinking this coffee

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:51.790
and making this coffee.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.550
And as we saw earlier, it
takes time to make this coffee.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:58.740
It's not an instant process, and
you really need to spend time.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.070
No life is going to go
completely horrendous,

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.060
and no life is going to
go without any problem.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:04.685
ALYSSA GOODMAN: Right.

00:10:04.685 --> 00:10:07.800
CENGIZ CEMALOGLU: So it's important
to highlight opportunities

